  "status": "Refused",
  "description": "Change of use from Public House (Sui generic) to place of worship (F1)",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "In the absence of an authoritative marketing report or robust community needs assessment, the proposal fails to demonstrate that all reasonable steps have been taken to retain the public house use. As such, the would result in the&nbsp;unjustified loss of the public house and is contrary to:Policy HC7 of the London Plan (March 2021)Policies SP4 and DMS3 of the Barking and Dagenham Local Plan (September 2024)The LBBD&nbsp;Last Orders? Preserving Public Houses SPD",
    "The proposal fails to provide sufficient information regarding the operational details of the place of worship/community centre, including expected user numbers, frequency of visits, timing of activities, and management measures. In the absence of this information, officers cannot be satisfied that the development would protect residential amenity, thus contrary to:National Planning Policy Framework (MHCLG, December 2024)Policy D6 and D14 of the London Plan (March 2021)Policy DMS3 of the Barking and Dagenham Local Plan (September 2024)&nbsp;",
    "The proposal fails to demonstrate adequate transport and parking arrangements. No Transport Statement, Car Parking Design and Management Plan, or Travel Plan has been submitted, and the plans do not show the layout, markings, or provision of parking spaces, disabled bays, cycle storage, or safe access and circulation. In the absence of trip generation data, modal split, or peak attendance figures, it is not possible to assess whether the proposed parking provision is sufficient. Given the site’s low PTAL rating and absence of parking controls, the development would result in excessive reliance on private vehicles, overspill parking, and consequent harm to highway safety and local amenity:National Planning Policy Framework (MHCLG, December 2024)Policies&nbsp;T1,&nbsp; T2,&nbsp; T5 and T6 of the London Plan (March 2021)Policies SP8, DMT1, DMT2 and DMT3 of the Barking and Dagenham Local Plan (September 2024)"
  ],
